@@FN@@
===
Throughout England generally, the tip of the tongue is quite free from the teeth, except for TH in *thin* and *then*, and for T and D it only touches the hard palate, seldom advancing so far as the root of the gums.—*Translator.*]

@@FN@@
>>>
<<<
@@FN@@
===
Footnote: † [This again is true for German, but not for English, French, or Italian, and not even for the adjacent Slavonic languages. In these languages the glottis is quite closed for both the mediae and the tenues in ordinary speech, @@FN@@
>>>
<<<
@@FN@@
===
but the voice begins for the mediae *before* releasing the closure of the lips or tongue and palate, and for the tenues *at the moment of release*. Although in giving vowel sounds, &c., I have generally contented myself with translating the same into English symbols and @@FN@@
>>>
<<<
@@FN@@
===
examples, it seemed better in the present case, where the author was speaking especially of the phenomena of speech to which he was personally accustomed, to leave the text unaltered and draw attention to English peculiarities in footnotes.—*Translator.*]

@@FN@@
