<<<
@@FN@@
===
Footnote: ‡ [Observe again that this description of the rush of wind accompanying P, T, K, although true for German habits of speech, is not true for the usual English habits, which require the windrush between the opening of the mouth and sounding of the vowel to be @@FN@@
>>>
<<<
@@FN@@
===
entirely suppressed. The English result is a gliding vowel sound preceding the true vowel on commencing a syllable, and following the vowel on ending one. The difference between English P and German P is precisely the same (as I have verified by actual observation) @@FN@@
>>>
<<<
@@FN@@
===
as that between the simple Sanscrit tenuis P, and the postaspirated Sanscrit Ph, as now actually pronounced by cultivated Bengalese. See my *Early English Pronunciation*, p. 1136, col. 1.—*Translator.*]
